---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "What difference does tech make? Conceptualizations of Disability and Assistive Technology among Kenyan Youth";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      The paper shows that, despite disproportionately higher disability rates in the Global South, there is still a gap in research focused on Global South contexts versus Global North contexts. One example is the lack of multilingual accessibility support in screen readers. [1]
    </p>

    <p>
      The authors note that access to assistive technologies (ATs) is often seen as access to status and money, which also holds true to some extent in the U.S. [2]
    </p>

    <p>
      Goffman describes stigma as “from a whole and usual person to a tainted, discounted one.” [3] In addition to the components of stigma discussed by the authors, I see stigma as the lack of consent of the individual who is put into a box and viewed only through a non-conforming trait in their community.
    </p>

    <p>
      The third component of stigma reminds me of the human tendency to default to binary thinking, which is harmful. [4] One example is feeling superior to disabled people, which can show up as pity, mocking, brushing off their experience, or a power imbalance.
    </p>

    <p>
      I agree that social and medical models (and many others) are often too narrow for the Global South and likely vary by region. The social and medical model developed initially does not sufficiently explain AT and disability in Kenya. Morality, superstition (positive or negative), corruption, GDP per capita, and the GINI Index affect accessibility. The GINI Index difference could also explain my earlier point that money affects access to ATs in the U.S.
    </p>

    <p>
      I think it was more productive for the authors to give participants freedom to chat in their own language, which captures richer behavioral cues and contextual knowledge. However, I think it could have been a video call via WhatsApp (depending on internet stability and participant willingness) to capture expressions too, since many countries have their own nuanced ways of expressing opinions. To further improve accuracy, it would also have helped to design the study prompts in the participants' native languages rather than just allowing responses in those languages. This reduces the chance that participants translate literally from English, which can be offensive or inaccurate.
    </p>

    <p>
      The overall more positive attitude toward ATs could be because they signal higher status, whereas disability is often pitied.
    </p>

    <p>
      The authors note that no participants mentioned how mainstream devices could be used as ATs, but one participant (P5, Coast Region, Female, 22) said, “I agree that social and medical models, along with many others, are often niche to the Global South and likely vary from region to region within it.” [5] I would have liked the authors to clarify whether their interpretation here was shaped by linguistic or contextual framing.
    </p>

    <p style="text-align: center;">
      Reference:
    </p>

    <p>
      [1] Masudul Hasan Masud Bhuiyan, Matteo Varvello, Yasir Zaki, and Cristian-Alexandru Staicu. 2025. Not All Visitors are Bilingual: A Measurement Study of the Multilingual Web from an Accessibility Perspective. In Proceedings of the 2025 ACM Internet Measurement Conference (IMC '25). Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, 871–881. https://doi.org/10.1145/3730567.3764505
    </p>

    <p>
      [2] Orellano-Colón, E. M., Rivero-Méndez, M., Ralat-Fonseca, B. N., Varas-Díaz, N., Lizama-Troncoso, M., Jiménez-Velázquez, I. Z., & Jutai, J. W. (2024). Multilevel barriers to using assistive technology devices among older hispanics from poor and disadvantaged communities: the relevance of a gender analysis. Disability and Rehabilitation: Assistive Technology, 19(3), 682–698. https://doi.org/10.1080/17483107.2022.2117427
    </p>

    <p>
      [3] Erving Goffman. 2009. Stigma: Notes on the management of spoiled identity. Simon and Schuster.
    </p>

    <p>
      [4] Simplican, S. C. (2017). Feminist Disability Studies as Methodology: Life-Writing and the Abled/Disabled Binary. Feminist Review, 115(1), 46-60. https://doi.org/10.1057/s41305-017-0039-x
    </p>

    <p>
      [5] Giulia Barbareschi, Norah Shitawa Kopi, Ben Oldfrey, and Catherine Holloway. 2021. What difference does tech make? Conceptualizations of Disability and Assistive Technology among Kenyan Youth: Conceptualizations of Disability and AT. In Proceedings of the 23rd International ACM SIGACCESS Conference on Computers and Accessibility (ASSETS '21). Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, Article 18, 1–13. https://doi.org/10.1145/3441852.3471226
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
